1: # The full Euler cube for the actual source shear
2: 
3: Complete working derivation for peer audit, 17 November 2026.
4: No statement number or result mapping is assigned by this draft.
5: We retain the selected-cusp positive rho loop, its actual source and
6: finite caps, the physical delta action, and the fans
7: \(T_n=T(e_1+ne_2,e_2)\), for every integer \(n\). All quotient-piece,
8: source, normal and cap maps used below are the literal maps of
9: P3K.63. We do not replace any of these maps by a comparison of their
10: homology groups.
11: 
12: ## EC.1. The real connection, the actual complex line, and orientation
13: 
14: We first fix the signs of a real circle connection explicitly. Let
15: \(\mathcal L_\delta\) be the complex line whose nonzero vectors are the
16: actual physical principal circle bundle. Its local fibre coefficient
17: is \(V=|V|\exp(2\pi i d)\); the physical delta action increases \(d\).
18: A real principal connection \(A\), normalized by
19: \(A(\partial_d)=1\), gives the unitary line connection \(2\pi i A\)
20: in local unit frames. Consequently
21: \[
22:  c_1(\mathcal L_\delta)
23:        =\frac{i}{2\pi}F_{\nabla_\delta}=-dA.
24:                                                         \tag{EC.1}
25: \]
26: For example, in the actual Cox coordinate
27: \(q_H=(1+jZ)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\), the physical action is
28: \(q_H\mapsto q_H e^{-2\pi i t}\). Its real connection is minus the
29: usual contact connection in that right-complex spinor. Formula
30: (EC.1) gives the positive meridian class retained in F56 and K42.
31: It is the same complex line as the source line on a regular source
32: slice: K6's literal page restriction is
33: \(x|_{\mathrm{page}}=c_1(M)\). We retain this arrow, rather than change
34: the associated representation of the circle.
35: 
36: It is convenient to perform the connection calculation on the inverse
37: line
38: \[
39:  \mathcal U=\mathcal L_\delta^{-1},\qquad
40:  \widehat x=c_1(\mathcal U)=-x=dA.
41:                                                         \tag{EC.2}
42: \]
43: Thus the real forms in SQ.12--15 represent \(\widehat x\) when the
44: principal coordinate is the increasing physical delta coefficient
45: \(d\). This makes the associated-line sign explicit. The vanishing
46: Pontryagin pairing of K63 is unaffected by replacing \(x\) by \(-x\);
47: its statement and relative-coordinate conclusions are unchanged.
48: 
49: We also keep the contracting suspension coefficient
50: \(\gamma=\gamma_B\). In physical coordinates
51: \(V=r\exp(2\pi i d)\), the complex threefold has orientation
52: \(\Omega_S\wedge dr\wedge dd\). Taking the physical delta circle
53: first gives orbit orientation
54: \(-\Omega_S\wedge d\log r\). The contracting coefficient increases
55: as \(\log r\) decreases. Hence its orbit orientation is
56: \(\Omega_S\wedge d\gamma_B\), as calculated in GO.1. The physical
57: positive-parameter completion therefore uses
58: \(\Omega_S\wedge d\gamma_B\wedge d\theta\). K42's actual parameter
59: reversal to the Jin completion reverses this orientation and carries
60: the same line \(\mathcal L_\delta\).
61: 
62: Write \(r_q\) for an increasing radial coordinate in the q disc and
63: \(\alpha=\arg(q)/(2\pi)\). The positive source orientation near its
64: regular boundary is
65: \[
66:  dr_q\wedge d\alpha\wedge du\wedge dv .
67:                                                         \tag{EC.3}
68: \]
69: Indeed the source fibre is \(u+\tau v\), with
70: \(\operatorname{Im}\tau>0\). The two reversals, of the Jin
71: orientation and of the odd cube \(x^3=(-\widehat x)^3\), cancel.
72: We can therefore compute the actual Jin integer \(k\) by integrating
73: the cube of the curvature of \(\mathcal U\) in the auxiliary
74: orientation
75: \[
76:  \Omega_+=dr_q\wedge d\alpha\wedge du\wedge dv
77:                          \wedge d\gamma_B\wedge d\theta .
78:                                                         \tag{EC.4}
79: \]
80: This is an equality of the specified oriented integrals. No integer
81: lift of a residue is used to choose its sign.
82: 
83: ## EC.2. Full periods and exact boundary line coordinates
84: 
85: Retain SQ.1--2 in full:
86: \[
87:  h:(Z,V)\mapsto(\kappa qZ,\alpha_L V),\qquad
88:  B:(Z,V)\mapsto(gZ,\eta qV),
89: \]
90: \[
91:  Z_n=Z,\quad V_n=Z^{-n}V,\quad h_n=hB^n,\quad B_n=B,
92: \]
93: \[
94:  Q_n=\kappa qg^n,\quad
95:  \alpha_{L,n}=\alpha_L\eta^n\kappa^{-n}g^{-n^2},
96:  \qquad\eta_n=\eta g^{-n}.
97:                                                         \tag{EC.5}
98: \]
99: The symbol \(\alpha_L\) here denotes the line multiplier; it is
100: not the angle \(\alpha\). The rho windings of
101: \((\kappa,\alpha_L,g,\eta)\) are \((-6,0,0,0)\).
102: The actual periodic logarithms of every zero-winding factor are
103: retained.
104: 
105: Here is a precise connection choice that retains them. On the regular
106: boundary, use the actual real period coefficients
107: \[
108:  (z,\ell)=u(1,0)+v(\tau,\mu)+d(0,1)+\gamma(G,\beta),
109:  \qquad z=\frac{\log Z}{2\pi i},\quad
110:         \ell=\frac{\log V}{2\pi i},
111:                                                         \tag{EC.6}
112: \]
113: where
114: \(\tau=\log(\kappa q)/(2\pi i)\),
115: \(\mu=\log\alpha_L/(2\pi i)\),
116: \(G=\log g/(2\pi i)\) and
117: \(\beta=\log(\eta q)/(2\pi i)\).
118: The imaginary parts give exactly the invertible matrix in SQ.4a;
119: the real parts then determine \(u,d\). In particular
120: \[
121:  d=\operatorname{Re}\ell-\operatorname{Re}\mu\,v
122:                            -\operatorname{Re}\beta\,\gamma.
123:                                                         \tag{EC.7}
124: \]
125: Differentiating (EC.7) retains the differentials and values of both
126: unit logarithms. Thus using \(d\) as a coefficient coordinate does
127: not set their phases or their derivatives to zero.
128: 
129: The q and rho coordinate changes are respectively
130: \[
131:  (u,d)\mapsto(u-v,d-\gamma),\qquad
132:  (u,d)\mapsto(u+6v,d).
133:                                                         \tag{EC.8}
134: \]
135: The complete zero-winding logarithms are single-valued and introduce
136: no further integer change. Consequently
137: \[
138:  A_0=dd+\alpha\,d\gamma
139:                                                         \tag{EC.9}
140: \]
141: is an actual global real principal connection on this boundary.
142: In physical \(V\) coordinates it contains all terms obtained by
143: differentiating (EC.7). It is a permissible connection choice on the
144: actual bundle, not an omission of its units.
145: 
146: Substitution in (EC.6), including both complex coordinates, gives
147: exactly
148: \[
149:  u_n=u,\quad v_n=v,\quad\gamma_n=\gamma-nv,\quad d_n=d-nu.
150:                                                         \tag{EC.10}
151: \]
152: In the new coordinates the corresponding real connection is
153: \[
154:  A_n=dd_n+\alpha\,d\gamma_n+6n\theta\,dv.
155:                                                         \tag{EC.11}
156: \]
157: Its rho change is \(d_n\mapsto d_n-6nv\), and its q change is
158: \(d_n\mapsto d_n-\gamma_n\), so (EC.11) is invariant under the
159: literal two deck arrows. Equations (EC.9)--(EC.11) give the exact
160: global difference
161: \[
162:  \beta_n=-n\{du+(\alpha-6\theta)dv\}.
163:                                                         \tag{EC.12}
164: \]
165: It is invariant under both changes (EC.8). If one uses the source
166: coframe \(du+(\alpha-6\theta+\phi_\kappa)dv\), then the additional
167: one-form \(n\phi_\kappa dv\) is present in (EC.12). It has not been
168: deleted. Formula (EC.7) explains the same fact for every other unit.
169: 
170: ## EC.3. The relative Cech line isomorphism
171: 
172: Let \(\mathcal C\) be the six-dimensional family of completed cusp
173: quotient pieces for \(T_0\), over the positive theta circle. Use
174: SQ.1--2 to identify each actual \(\mathcal C_n\) with this fixed
175: smooth piece. Those arrows are periodic, preserve the outer real
176: period marking, and retain the typed Cartier spinor map and the
177: physical Hopf cap. Its boundary is denoted \(\mathcal B\).
178: 
179: Choose the graph map \(b:\mathcal C\to\mathbb R/\mathbb Z\) of
180: SQ.3, with boundary value \(v\). The same proof modifies it by a
181: supported real function to be constant near the fixed tube and
182: extends it over the cap. It is periodic in theta. Define an actual
183: complex line \(\mathcal T\) on \(\mathcal C\) by cutting theta
184: and gluing its coefficient by
185: \[
186:  (\theta,p,w)\longmapsto
187:        (\theta+1,p,e^{2\pi i b(p)}w).
188:                                                         \tag{EC.13}
189: \]
190: In these cut unit frames its connection is
191: \(\nabla_{\mathcal T}=d-2\pi i\theta\,db\). The frame change at
192: theta one is the inverse of the displayed coefficient change, so
193: this connection descends exactly. Its Chern curvature is
194: \[
195:                          \Xi=d\theta\wedge db,\qquad \Xi^2=0.
196:                                                         \tag{EC.14}
197: \]
198: 
199: The actual normalization constant factor
200: \(\alpha_{L,n}/\alpha_L\) has rho winding \(6n\). In the inverse
201: line \(\mathcal U\), its Chern class is consequently
202: \(+6n[d\theta]\smile b\). The other classes of the line difference
203: are zero: the typed SQ.8 Hopf arrow preserves the meridian value;
204: the regular \(b\times\gamma\) torus has the integral period section
205: from SQ.3; and the gamma times theta value is zero because
206: \(\eta_n/\eta=g^{-n}\) has its periodic logarithm. The integral
207: Thom, Mayer--Vietoris and Wang calculation SQ.16 has no torsion
208: and shows that these are all the evaluations. Thus
209: \[
210:  c_1(\mathcal U_n)-c_1(\mathcal U_0)
211:                    =6n\,c_1(\mathcal T)
212:              \quad\hbox{on }\mathcal C.               \tag{EC.15}
213: \]
214: This is also the direct theta-cut/graph-cut clutch square: the
215: physical delta coefficient changes by \(-6n b\), so its dual
216: coefficient changes by \(+6n b\), exactly (EC.13).
217: The zero-winding logarithms left in (EC.5) are
218: \[
219:  nf_\eta-nf_\kappa-n^2f_g,\qquad -nf_g,
220:                                                         \tag{EC.16}
221: \]
222: together with the actual source-flow pullbacks of their extending
223: units. They are retained in the bundle whose class is (EC.15).
224: 
225: We next prove the stronger relative assertion with its prescribed
226: outer line arrow:
227: \[
228:  J_n:\mathcal U_n\longrightarrow
229:                     \mathcal U_0\otimes\mathcal T^{6n}.
230:                                                         \tag{EC.17}
231: \]
232: On \(\mathcal B\), this arrow is specified in the doubly cut real
233: period frames by (EC.10)--(EC.11) and the clutch (EC.13).
234: Their deck changes agree exactly, so it is a genuine boundary
235: bundle isomorphism. The assertion is that this particular boundary
236: arrow extends, including its integral gauge; an absolute equality
237: of Chern classes alone would not prove that assertion.
238: 
239: Here are the exact groups and the only possible relative obstruction.
240: On \(\mathcal B\), the q monodromy has commutator \(u\) with the
241: source graph loop \(v\); its coefficient is one. The rho monodromy
242: has commutator \(6u\), and all other retained circle generators
243: commute after abelianization. Hence
244: \[
245:  H_1(\mathcal B;\mathbb Z)
246:        =\mathbb Z\langle\alpha,\theta,v,\gamma\rangle.
247:                                                         \tag{EC.18}
248: \]
249: The \(u\) generator has been killed primitively, so no finite
250: summand has been suppressed. SQ.16 and its periodic graph and
251: gamma maps give
252: \[
253:  H^1(\mathcal C;\mathbb Z)
254:        =\mathbb Z\langle d\theta,db,d\gamma\rangle,
255: \quad
256:  \operatorname{im}H^1(\mathcal C)
257:        =\mathbb Z\langle d\theta,dv,d\gamma\rangle
258:                    \subset H^1(\mathcal B).           \tag{EC.19}
259: \]
260: The gamma map extends through the cap by its real lift near the
261: fixed sphere, exactly as for \(b\). A section of the theta
262: family outside the fixed tube splits the Wang sequence, so there
263: is no extra generator or torsion in (EC.19). Therefore the only
264: boundary winding that does not extend is the q-angle winding.
265: 
266: We test it using an actual disc, rather than a rational cohomology
267: argument. Choose a constant Tate section \(Z=Z_*\), with
268: \(|Z_*|=1\), at a smooth normalization point avoiding the
269: marked zero section, the retained Picard-section divisors and
270: the two nodal branches. This can be done uniformly: the explicit
271: Tate points of F54 remain near \(1\) for the chosen small rho
272: circle, whereas a point near \(-1\) stays disjoint from them;
273: shrink the common q disc once more if necessary. Take the marked gamma
274: value zero. The fixed graph's short arc in SQ.4c is centred at
275: one half, so this value lies outside it. The section in the
276: source neighbourhood extends across q zero through a smooth
277: point of the nodal fibre. Together with this gamma value it is
278: a disc in the orbit piece, disjoint from the fixed tube and its
279: surgery; it therefore lies in the completed piece as well.
280: The SQ.2 source horizontal field can be taken to preserve this
281: section. On the outside collar its real marked-period field is
282: already zero on \(v=0\); near the node the section is disjoint.
283: Adding this prescribed zero field to the stated partition of
284: unity leaves all the node, boundary and cap arrows unchanged.
285: 
286: More generally, at a fixed gamma value in this free interval the
287: suspension-untwisted coordinates give the physical source point
288: \[
289:              Z_{\rm phys}=Z_*\exp(\gamma\log g).
290:                                                         \tag{EC.20}
291: \]
292: This is a nonzero extending function on the q disc; its logarithm
293: uses the actual periodic \(\log g\). The line character arrow
294: on it is literally multiplication by \(Z_{\rm phys}^{-n}\).
295: The new untwisting factor
296: \(\eta_n^\gamma=\eta^\gamma\exp(-n\gamma\log g)\)
297: cancels the displayed \(g\) factor on passing to the common
298: untwisted line frame. Its remaining factor is \(Z_*^{-n}\).
299: Equivalently one can retain both factors in (EC.20); both extend
300: and are nowhere zero. No fractional power of \(q\) is used in
301: this ratio: the common \(q^\gamma\) factors cancel before the
302: ratio is formed. The disc lies in one free suspension interval,
303: so no B reindex is introduced. The tensor line \(\mathcal T\)
304: is trivial on this disc at fixed theta and \(b=v=0\).
305: For the chosen value gamma zero, the untwisting is literally the
306: identity: the disc is the ordinary source section with \(V\) in
307: one fixed nonzero line chart. We can take \(V=1\). In the two
308: actual first vertex charts this disc and its image have coordinates
309: \[
310:  (q/Z_*,Z_*,1)\longmapsto
311:                  (qZ_*^{\,n-1},Z_*,Z_*^{-n}).
312:                                                         \tag{EC.20a}
313: \]
314: Both charts have physical height equal to the product of their
315: three coordinates. Their second and third coordinates are nonzero
316: at q zero, so this is the free locus and no fixed-tube or cap
317: coordinate is involved. The inverse is given by the opposite
318: nonzero powers of \(Z_*\). The dual line arrow is multiplication
319: by \(Z_*^{-n}\). This gives an extending isomorphism
320: with the unchanged physical delta frame. The prescribed boundary
321: arrow in (EC.17) thus extends over this disc by this explicit line
322: map. Its q-angle winding is zero.
323: 
324: For completeness, an explicit Cech construction turns these facts
325: into the full isomorphism. On a good cover of \(\mathcal C\), use
326: the actual unitary transition functions of
327: \(\mathcal R_n=\mathcal U_n\otimes\mathcal U_0^{-1}
328: \otimes\mathcal T^{-6n}\), formed from all the retained maps.
329: Choose real logarithms on the overlaps. Their Cech coboundary
330: is an integral two-cocycle. Equation (EC.15) says that cocycle
331: is the coboundary of an integral one-cochain; subtract that
332: one-cochain from the chosen logarithms. Our local frame convention
333: is \(e_j=\exp(2\pi i r_{ij})e_i\). The resulting real
334: one-cocycle \(r_{ij}\) is solved by
335: \[
336:                   a_i=\sum_k\rho_k r_{ki},\qquad
337:                   a_j-a_i=r_{ij},                    \tag{EC.21}
338: \]
339: for a partition of unity subordinate to the cover. Multiplying
340: the local unit frames by \(\exp(-2\pi i a_i)\) gives a global
341: unit frame. Since
342: \(\mathcal R_n=\operatorname{Hom}
343: (\mathcal U_0\otimes\mathcal T^{6n},\mathcal U_n)\),
344: this frame is a nowhere-zero map in that direction. Its inverse
345: is the absolute isomorphism \(J_n\) in (EC.17).
346: Thus the direction of the required map is fixed by the actual
347: transitions, including (EC.16).
348: 
349: Compare its boundary value with the prescribed boundary arrow.
350: Their ratio is a circle-valued function. By the section-disc
351: calculation its alpha coefficient is zero. By (EC.18)--(EC.19)
352: its other three winding coefficients are integral multiples
353: of the restrictions of theta, \(b\), and gamma. Multiply the
354: absolute isomorphism by the corresponding extending functions
355: \(\exp(2\pi i(a\theta+cb+d\gamma))\) to remove these coefficients.
356: The remaining boundary ratio has a single real logarithm.
357: Extend that logarithm to the collar, multiply it by a cutoff,
358: and set it to zero in the remaining interior. Multiplication
359: by its exponential makes the boundary map exactly the
360: prescribed one. This proves (EC.17) as a relative bundle map.
361: It retains every possible boundary integer rather than declaring
362: the zero-winding unit terms harmless without an extending arrow.
363: 
364: The cap is included in this construction. Its actual line is
365: the one carried by the SQ.8 Cartier/Hopf map. The supported
366: change \(b-b'\) is implemented on the theta cut by its explicit
367: exponential in SQ.3. A residual circle gauge on the inner cap
368: boundary has only its theta winding, and this extends over
369: \(D^3\times S^2\times S^1_\theta\). Thus no additional cap
370: line class or unspecified framing is inserted in (EC.17).
371: 
372: ## EC.4. The affine curvature family and the oriented collar integral
373: 
374: Choose a connection on \(\mathcal U_0\) whose boundary value is
375: the one just fixed, and write its real Chern curvature as \(F_0\).
376: On the fixed completed piece use the relative isomorphism
377: (EC.17) and the tensor connection of (EC.13). Their curvature is
378: exactly
379: \[
380:                          F_n=F_0+6n\Xi .
381:                                                         \tag{EC.22}
382: \]
383: All connections here are on the actual lines. They need not be
384: Chern connections of the original complex structure; Chern--Weil
385: evaluation is independent of that choice. The relative map
386: (EC.17) ensures their specified boundary values agree with
387: the actual outer gluing. In particular no omitted Mayer--Vietoris
388: gauge can add a further quadratic or other term.
389: 
390: Keep the original connection on the common exterior, where the
391: fan change is absent. In piece coordinates its pullback on
392: \(\mathcal B\) is displayed below. From this point onward, after
393: pullback by the actual piece map, we write \(\gamma=\gamma_n\)
394: for the common piece coefficient. The exterior coefficient is
395: \(\gamma_{\rm ext}=\gamma+nv\), and its delta coefficient is
396: \(d_{\rm ext}=d_n+nu\). Thus the \(\gamma\) in the curvature
397: formula is the new piece coefficient, not the exterior coefficient.
398: With this dictionary the literal pullback is
399: \[
400:  A_{\mathrm{ext},n}
401:    =dd_n+\alpha\,d\gamma_n+n(du+\alpha\,dv),
402: \quad
403:  F_{\mathrm{ext},n}
404:    =d\alpha\wedge d\gamma+n\,d\alpha\wedge dv.
405:                                                         \tag{EC.23}
406: \]
407: The difference between the piece and exterior connections is
408: the global form (EC.12). It has derivative
409: \[
410:  d\beta_n=-n\,d\alpha\wedge dv+6n\,d\theta\wedge dv .
411:                                                         \tag{EC.24}
412: \]
413: Directly,
414: \[
415:  F_{\mathrm{ext},n}^2=0,\quad (d\beta_n)^2=0,
416: \quad F_{\mathrm{ext},n}+d\beta_n
417:             =d\alpha\wedge d\gamma+6n\,d\theta\wedge dv .
418:                                                         \tag{EC.25}
419: \]
420: 
421: Orient \(\mathcal B\) as the outward boundary of the piece in
422: (EC.4):
423: \[
424:  \Omega_{\mathcal B}
425:        =d\alpha\wedge du\wedge dv\wedge d\gamma\wedge d\theta .
426:                                                         \tag{EC.26}
427: \]
428: Each marked angular coordinate has period one. Its integral
429: over the actual boundary is one: the q/rho monodromies shear
430: \(u\) by integral multiples of \(v\), and hence preserve this
431: volume form and its unit fundamental cell. The full unit
432: periods have already entered the coordinate construction
433: (EC.6); they do not alter this cell or its primitive lattice.
434: 
435: Let \(t\) increase across a gluing collar from the piece toward
436: the exterior. Its orientation is \(dt\wedge\Omega_{\mathcal B}\).
437: Interpolate by
438: \[
439:  A(t)=A_{\mathrm{ext},n}+\chi(t)\beta_n,\qquad
440:                   \chi(0)=1,\quad\chi(1)=0.
441:                                                         \tag{EC.27}
442: \]
443: It is constant near each endpoint. The curvature and its cube
444: are
445: \[
446:  F=F_{\mathrm{ext},n}+\chi d\beta_n
447:                            +\chi' dt\wedge\beta_n,
448: \]
449: \[
450:  F^3=3\chi'\,dt\wedge
451:           \beta_n\wedge(F_{\mathrm{ext},n}+\chi d\beta_n)^2 .
452:                                                         \tag{EC.28}
453: \]
454: The term without \(dt\) vanishes on the five-dimensional boundary.
455: The exact exterior-algebra identity is
456: \[
457:  3\int_0^1
458:    \beta_n\wedge(F_{\mathrm{ext},n}+s\,d\beta_n)^2\,ds
459:                     =18n^2\,\Omega_{\mathcal B}.        \tag{EC.29}
460: \]
461: The actual interpolation in (EC.27) runs from \(s=1\) to \(s=0\).
462: Its contribution is therefore \(-18n^2\), with the sign fixed
463: by (EC.26)--(EC.28).
464: 
465: The interior contribution is linear because \(\Xi^2=0\):
466: \[
467:  F_n^3-F_0^3=18n\,F_0^2\wedge\Xi .
468:                                                         \tag{EC.30}
469: \]
470: The common exterior contributes no change. Its boundary map
471: has already been accounted for by (EC.23)--(EC.29).
472: There is consequently one real constant \(L\), independent
473: of \(n\), such that the actual integer Euler cubes satisfy
474: \[
475:                          k_n=k_0+Ln-18n^2 .
476:                                                         \tag{EC.31}
477: \]
478: This conclusion follows from the global relative line map
479: and the actual oriented gluing. It is not a polynomial assigned
480: from the local fifth form alone.
481: 
482: ## EC.5. Evaluation of the linear term and exact consequences
483: 
484: K42 proves \(k_0=6\). The fan \(T_1\) is literally the reflected
485: fan of K46: its first triangle is
486: \((0,e_1+e_2,e_2)\), and translating its second triangle by
487: \(-e_2\) gives \((0,e_1,e_1+e_2)\). The translation is the
488: actual B period, including its full unit map, as retained in
489: K56. K46 constructs the oriented cap and line isomorphism for
490: that reflected family and proves \(k_1=6\). Thus (EC.31) at
491: one gives \(L=18\), without any use of a residue.
492: 
493: The exact integer result is
494: \[
495:               k_n=6-18n(n-1),\qquad \ell_n=0,\qquad
496:               A_n=1-3n(n-1).
497:                                                         \tag{EC.32}
498: \]
499: Here \(\ell_n=0\) is the already proved K63 pairing, and
500: \(24A_n=4k_n-\ell_n\) is the fixed Jin normalization. The
501: K63 native and ordinary identities now give exact integers:
502: \[
503:  (m_0^{\rm native},n_0^{\rm native})
504:                   =(0,-6+18n(n-1)),
505: \]
506: \[
507:                  V_{00}=-18n(n-1),\qquad
508:                  \delta_0=-12+18n(n-1).
509:                                                         \tag{EC.33}
510: \]
511: Their reductions agree with K56 and K63. That agreement is a
512: check on the completed calculation, not its proof of an integer lift.
513: 
514: The exterior-form diagnostic remains preserved unchanged.
515: The new certificate, \path{code/part3/source_shear_euler_cube.py},
516: passes 41 exact checks of the actual
517: period-coordinate arrow, all integral H1 quotients, the line
518: connection deck invariance, the cubic collar identity with its
519: endpoint direction, the reflected triangle relabelling, and the
520: resulting exact polynomial. The geometric Cech, source-disc,
521: normal and cap arguments above are separate from that algebraic
522: certificate.
